Let $n \geq 2$. An entire function $F:\mathbb{C}^n \to \mathbb{C}$ is said to have bounded $L$-index in a direction $\mathbf{b} \in \mathbb{C}^n \setminus \{0\}$ on a set $G \subseteq \mathbb{C}^n$, where $L:\mathbb{C}^n \to (0,\infty)$ is a positive continuous function, if there exists a nonnegative integer $m_0$ such that for every $z \in G$,
$$ \frac{1}{m!\, L(z)^{m}} \left| \partial^{m}_{\mathbf{b}} F(z) \right| \;\le\; \max_{0 \le k \le m_0}\, \frac{1}{k!\, L(z)^{k}} \left| \partial^{k}_{\mathbf{b}} F(z) \right| \qquad \text{for all } m \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}, $$
where $\partial^{k}_{\mathbf{b}} F(z) = \frac{d^{k}}{dt^{k}} F(z+t\mathbf{b}) \big|_{t=0}$ denotes the $k$-th directional derivative of $F$ along $\mathbf{b}$.

Construct (or prove the impossibility of constructing) the following data:
- an entire function $F:\mathbb{C}^n \to \mathbb{C}$;
- a positive continuous function $L:\mathbb{C}^n \to (0,\infty)$;
- two disjoint unbounded domains $G_1, G_2 \subseteq \mathbb{C}^n$ with $G_1 \cup G_2 = \mathbb{C}^n$;
- two directions $\mathbf{b}_1, \mathbf{b}_2 \in \mathbb{C}^n \setminus \{0\}$;

such that $F$ has bounded $L$-index in direction $\mathbf{b}_1$ on $G_1$ and bounded $L$-index in direction $\mathbf{b}_2$ on $G_2$, but $F$ does not have bounded $L$-index in direction $\mathbf{b}_2$ on $G_1$ and does not have bounded $L$-index in direction $\mathbf{b}_1$ on $G_2$ (i.e., no single integer $m_0$ makes the corresponding inequality hold throughout $G_1$ with direction $\mathbf{b}_2$, nor throughout $G_2$ with direction $\mathbf{b}_1$).

